-- title:
--   Maximality of the Hecke eigenvalue ideal over a finite field
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a finite field and let $a$ assign to each prime number an element $a_\ell \in k$. The abstract Hecke algebra is the polynomial ring `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb{Z}[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$, the multivariate polynomial ring over $\mathbb{Z}$ whose variables are indexed by the primes, and the eigenvalue ideal [`ModularCurve.eigenIdeal a`](def/HeckeGalois_EichlerShimura.html#L30) is by definition the kernel of the $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}[X_\ell] \to k$ obtained by evaluating the variable indexed by $\ell$ at $a_\ell$ (the map `MvPolynomial.aeval a`). The theorem asserts that this ideal is a maximal ideal of `HeckeAlg`. Note that no modularity or Hecke-theoretic hypothesis on the system $(a_\ell)_\ell$ is imposed: the only assumptions are that $k$ is a field and that $k$ is finite, so that every system of values in a finite field gives rise to a maximal ideal of the abstract Hecke algebra, with residue field the subfield of $k$ generated by the $a_\ell$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that the ideal $\mathfrak{m}_a$ cut out by a mod-$\lambda$ eigensystem is maximal, so that $\mathbb{T}/\mathfrak{m}_a$ is a finite field and $\mathfrak{m}_a$-torsion of a Jacobian is a vector space over it. It is used in the residual Eichler–Shimura part of the development, by [`ModularCurve.eigenformSupportAt_jZero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eigenformSupportAt_jZero) and by [`ModularCurve.exists_isMaximal_heckeTorsion_jZero_ne_bot_of_ringHom_heckeAlgebra_two`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_isMaximal_heckeTorsion_jZero_ne_bot_of_ringHom_heckeAlgebra_two).
